-- statement:
--   This file builds a family of recursive-potential norms on finitely supported functions on finite rooted trees and states, as one defined proposition MainClaim, a bundle of results about the resulting spaces; it is not an established theorem. A finite rooted tree has a root, a parent map fixing the root, and a depth function with depth 0 at the root and strictly smaller depth for the parent of every non-root node. Given a vector v on nodes, leastPair(x,a,b) = (max(a, x+b), max(b, x−a)), and the potentials (P,Q) at a node are defined recursively from the leaves up: P(s) = max(a, v(s)+b) and Q(s) = max(b, −v(s)+a), where a and b are the square roots of the sums of the squares of the children's P-values and Q-values respectively. A variable version replaces the square-root aggregation by the ℓ^p aggregation (Σ|f|^p)^(1/p) with a child exponent p ≥ 1. The root sum P(root)+Q(root), computed on the initial subtree generated by the support, is used as a norm on finitely supported vectors; because the source does not prove the norm axioms, it assumes them, together with the coordinate bound |v(s)| ≤ norm and norm-decreasing projections onto initial sets, through hypotheses (Heads, Construction, LpConstruction classes), and MainClaim asserts these assumptions are satisfiable. The tree spaces are the completions. Two trees are used: sequenceTree, whose nodes are finite lists of naturals with parent the list minus its last entry, and joinedTree, a root joined to copies of the trees of lists of length at most h for every positive integer h. XZero is the completion of the root-coordinate-zero subspace, with norm P(root), assumed to be half the ambient norm. Using the modulus averagedModulus (the infimum over unit x of the supremum over cofinite closed subspaces F of the infimum of (‖x+ty‖+‖x−ty‖)/2 − 1 over y in F with ‖y‖ ≥ 1), and the AUC property (positive modulus for every t > 0) for equivalent norms, MainClaim asserts: completeness, infinite dimensionality, continuous coordinates and potentials, root formulas and projection properties for these spaces; reflexivity of XZero and XJoined; the lower bound t³/128 ≤ averagedModulus for 0 < t < 1; a cubic-defect inequality for the zero space; failure of AUC for every equivalent norm on XSigma, XZero and XJoined; positivity of averagedModulus on XSigma for all radii; a separated-sequence estimate on XJoined with explicit η from logarithmicGamma; and, for the ℓ^(1+1/h)-aggregated height-h component spaces and their ℓ²-sum Xv, completeness, reflexivity, separable dual, norm equivalences with ℓ^p, an exact ℓ^p potential model, and stability, weak-tail and zero-modulus estimates with explicit constants.
